"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][26]={"university_physics_en:M26-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M26-L1 – Hilbert spaces, self-adjoint operators and spectral decomposition<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Master: Advanced mathematical methods and model foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · methodological specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M8: linear operators and eigenvalue\nproblems; M12: quantum mechanics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish symmetry from\nself-adjointness, apply spectral projectors to a finite model and\nexplain why a discrete eigenbasis is not always available.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>A quantum observable is not always described adequately by a list of\neigenvectors. An orthonormal eigenbasis suffices in finite dimensions;\nposition and momentum operators on infinite-dimensional spaces may\nexhibit continuous spectra. The rigorous framework combines a Hilbert\nspace, a specified operator domain and a projection-valued spectral\nmeasure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>A complex Hilbert space is a complete inner-product space. The domain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis essential: an operator is self-adjoint when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=A^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincluding equality of the domains. Showing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\phi,A\\psi\\rangle=\\langle A\\phi,\\psi\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non a dense domain establishes symmetry, not necessarily\nself-adjointness. For self-adjoint operators the spectral theorem\nsupplies orthogonal projectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\Delta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassociated with measurable energy sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSumming over discrete eigenvectors is a special case.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><\/msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Pr<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda\\,\\mathrm dP(\\lambda),\\qquad f(A)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(\\lambda)\\,\\mathrm dP(\\lambda),\\qquad \\Pr(A\\in\\Delta)=\\langle\\psi,P(\\Delta)\\psi\\rangle.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>In a two-dimensional Hilbert space take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\operatorname{diag}(1,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand normalized\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi=(\\sqrt3/2,1/2)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe projector for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P([0,2])=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe probability is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the expectation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle A\\rangle=(3/4)\\cdot1+(1/4)\\cdot3=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure contrasts discrete lines with a possible continuum\nschematically; it is not a spectrum of this two-level matrix.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_268_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic spectrum with two discrete spectral lines and a continuous region, which need not occur together in any particular model.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic spectrum with two discrete\nspectral lines and a continuous region, which need not occur together in\nany particular model.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Discrete eigenvalues give individual lines; the projection-valued\nformulation can also represent a continuum.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Pr<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pr(A\\in[2,4])<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the example.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle A^2\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the same state.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does symmetry on a dense domain not automatically imply\nself-adjointness?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle A^2\\rangle=(3/4)\\cdot1+(1/4)\\cdot9=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The adjoint may have a larger domain; operator equality includes\nmatching domains.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The numerical model is finite-dimensional. Unbounded operators\nrequire a specified\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand integrability conditions for spectral calculus; the figure is not an\nexperimental spectrum.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Spectral projectors replace the unjustified universal assumption of a\ndiscrete eigenbasis. The next lesson applies equally careful definitions\nto curvilinear coordinates. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-102-introduction-to-functional-analysis-spring-2021/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Introduction to Functional Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Hilbert spaces and spectral theory; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L1 – Hilbert spaces, self-adjoint operators and spectral decomposition · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-322-quantum-theory-ii-spring-2003/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Quantum Theory II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · operators, symmetries and quantum models; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L1 – Hilbert spaces, self-adjoint operators and spectral decomposition · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M26-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M26-L2 – Manifolds, metrics and covariant derivatives<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Master: Advanced mathematical methods and model foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · methodological specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9: Lagrangian mechanics; M8: partial\nderivatives; M20: spatial coordinates. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Write the polar-coordinate\nmetric, calculate its connection coefficients and distinguish nonzero\nconnection coefficients from intrinsic curvature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>A trajectory may be straight in Cartesian coordinates yet appear\ncurved in polar coordinates. This is a coordinate effect, not physical\ncurvature. Geometric equations must transform consistently under changes\nof coordinates. The metric specifies distances, while the connection\ndescribes how vector components are differentiated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>On the Euclidean plane for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe line element is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds^2=dr^2+r^2d\\varphi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{rr}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\varphi\\varphi}=r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Christoffel symbols of the Levi-Civita connection follow from first\nderivatives of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey need not vanish in polar coordinates even though the intrinsic\nGaussian curvature of the plane is zero. Geodesics obey a\ncoordinate-invariant statement with extra connection terms in the\ncoordinate equation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>k<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>r<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma^k_{ij}=\\frac12 g^{k\\ell}(\\partial_i g_{j\\ell}+\\partial_j g_{i\\ell}-\\partial_\\ell g_{ij}),\\quad \\Gamma^r_{\\varphi\\varphi}=-r,\\quad \\Gamma^\\varphi_{r\\varphi}=\\frac1r.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a circle of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan angular interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\varphi=0.30\\,\\mathrm{rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsubtends an arc length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=r\\Delta\\varphi=0.60\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA radial straight line at constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a geodesic; a circle at constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a free geodesic of the flat plane. The figure describes geometry\nrather than a gravitational field.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_060_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Polar-coordinate diagram with radius, angle, circular arc and tangent on a flat plane.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Polar-coordinate diagram with radius,\nangle, circular arc and tangent on a flat plane.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The changing basis in polar coordinates generates connection\nterms even without intrinsic curvature.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dr=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\varphi=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>r<\/mi><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma^r_{\\varphi\\varphi}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does a nonzero Christoffel symbol imply curved space?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.03<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ds=r\\,d\\varphi=0.03\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin these coordinates.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; the coefficients depend on coordinates, whereas curvature is\ntensorial.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The model treats the two-dimensional Euclidean plane away from the\npolar-coordinate singularity. It does not derive Einstein’s field\nequations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Metric and connection separate measurable distances from\ncoordinate-dependent derivatives. Their symmetries lead naturally to\ncontinuous transformation groups. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/\">David Tong – General Relativity<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · manifolds, metric and covariant differentiation; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L2 – Manifolds, metrics and covariant derivatives · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M26-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M26-L3 – Lie groups, infinitesimal symmetries and conserved quantities<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Master: Advanced mathematical methods and model foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · methodological specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9: Noether’s theorem; M12: angular\nmomentum; M26-L2: coordinates and vectors. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a generator from\ncontinuous rotations and obtain a conservation law from a rotationally\ninvariant Lagrangian.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>A symmetry is more than a visually unchanged picture: it relates\nphysically equivalent descriptions. Continuous symmetries form\ndifferentiable groups under suitable assumptions. Their derivatives at\nthe identity are generators that describe infinitesimal\ntransformations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Plane rotations are represented by matrices\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA finite rotation can be written as the exponential of an antisymmetric\ngenerator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a free particle or one in a central potential the angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis cyclic: the Lagrangian has no explicit dependence on it. The\nEuler–Lagrange equation then implies conservation of the conjugate\nmomentum.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mover><mi>r<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\theta)=e^{\\theta J},\\quad J=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad L=\\frac m2(\\dot r^2+r^2\\dot\\varphi^2)-V(r),\\quad p_\\varphi=mr^2\\dot\\varphi=\\mathrm{const}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a particle starts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_1=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varphi_1=0.5\\,\\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf angular momentum is conserved and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_2=1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varphi_2=\\dot\\varphi_1(r_1/r_2)^2=2\\,\\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlso,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\pi/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntakes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure illustrates the group orbit of a unit vector.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_151_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Circular orbit of two vectors related by a rotation through an angle.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Circular orbit of two vectors related by\na rotation through an angle.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The generator gives the infinitesimal change along the orbit of\nrotations.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\pi)(1,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if the radius is halved at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What assumption guarantees conservation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Angular velocity becomes four times larger.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The Lagrangian must be independent of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the Euler–Lagrange equations must apply.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Plane rotations form an abelian group; many three-dimensional and\nquantum symmetry groups are noncommutative. Noether statements require\nappropriate variational principles and boundary assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Continuous symmetries connect generators with conservation laws.\nVariational principles also give access to semiclassical approximations.\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-322-quantum-theory-ii-spring-2003/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Quantum Theory II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · operators, symmetries and quantum models; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L3 – Lie groups, infinitesimal symmetries and conserved quantities · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-238-geometry-and-quantum-field-theory-spring-2023/pages/syllabus/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Geometry and Quantum Field Theory, syllabus<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · variational and geometric methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L3 – Lie groups, infinitesimal symmetries and conserved quantities · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M26-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M26-L4 – Variational principles and the stationary-phase method<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Master: Advanced mathematical methods and model foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · methodological specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M8: Fourier methods and integration;\nM9: action principles; M12: wavefunctions. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Find stationary points of a\nphase and evaluate the leading contribution to a one-dimensional\noscillatory integral.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>Rapidly oscillating integrals often exhibit cancellation among\ncontributions with different phases. Near points where the first\nderivative of the phase vanishes, local phase variation is slower and\ncontributions may dominate. This connects variational principles to\nsemiclassical approximations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(\\lambda)=\\int a(x)e^{i\\lambda S(x)}dx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor large positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNear an isolated interior point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\prime(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\prime\\prime(x_0)\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nexpand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto quadratic order. The leading contribution follows from an oscillatory\nGaussian integral. Endpoints and multiple stationary points require\nfurther terms; degenerate points have different scaling laws.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∼<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sgn<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(\\lambda)\\sim a(x_0)\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda\\lvert S\\prime\\prime(x_0)\\rvert}}\\exp\\!\\left[i\\lambda S(x_0)+\\frac{i\\pi}{4}\\operatorname{sgn}S\\prime\\prime(x_0)\\right].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(x)=x^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(x)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover the whole real line, introducing a positive Gaussian regulator and\nthen removing it. The only stationary point is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\prime\\prime=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe regularized Fresnel integral tends to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msqrt><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=e^{i\\pi/4}\\sqrt{\\pi/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts magnitude is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.253<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.253<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand its phase is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure separates the phase profile from its oscillatory real\nexponential.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_279_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Parabolic phase above the rapidly oscillating real part of its complex exponential.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Parabolic phase above the rapidly\noscillating real part of its complex exponential.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The phase varies slowly near its stationary point; away from it\nrapid oscillations tend to cancel.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Locate the stationary point of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(x)=3(x-2)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the leading one-dimensional magnitude scale with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does the standard formula apply when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\prime\\prime(x_0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">As\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda^{-1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a nondegenerate interior point.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; higher-order terms of the phase must be considered.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The formula requires a sufficiently smooth amplitude, a nondegenerate\nstationary point and suitable integration conditions. The whole-line\nFresnel example with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a regulated limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Stationary phase provides a controlled approximation for oscillatory\nintegrals. Multidimensional Gaussian integrals underpin many discretized\npath integrals. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-238-geometry-and-quantum-field-theory-spring-2023/pages/syllabus/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Geometry and Quantum Field Theory, syllabus<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · variational and geometric methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L4 – Variational principles and the stationary-phase method · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-322-quantum-theory-ii-spring-2003/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Quantum Theory II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · operators, symmetries and quantum models; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L4 – Variational principles and the stationary-phase method · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M26-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M26-L5 – Gaussian functional integrals and discretized field models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Master: Advanced mathematical methods and model foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · methodological specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: partition functions; M26-L4:\nstationary phase; linear algebra. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate a finite Gaussian\nintegral, determine its covariance and identify the additional issues\nposed by continuum functional integrals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>Field theory and statistical physics often speak of integrating over\nall configurations. Such notation is initially formal. Control can be\ngained by discretizing a spatial field into finitely many degrees of\nfreedom and working with ordinary multidimensional integrals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Let a real field have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndiscrete degrees of freedom with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_N(x)=\\tfrac12x^TAx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis symmetric positive definite. The Euclidean weight\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-S_N(x)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndefines a normalizable Gaussian measure. Orthogonal diagonalization\ndecomposes the integral into independent normal modes. The matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the two-point covariance. A genuine continuum functional integral\nrequires additional work on measures, limits and often\nrenormalization.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msup><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_N=\\int_{\\mathbb R^N}e^{-\\frac12x^TAx}\\,\\mathrm d^Nx=\\frac{(2\\pi)^{N/2}}{\\sqrt{\\det A}},\\qquad \\langle x_ix_j\\rangle=(A^{-1})_{ij}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\operatorname{diag}(2,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\det A=16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_2=(2\\pi)/4=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe covariances are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle x_1^2\\rangle=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle x_2^2\\rangle=1/8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle x_1x_2\\rangle=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows level sets of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe narrow direction has the smaller variance.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_112_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Elliptical level sets of a two-dimensional quadratic action with Gaussian widths differing in two directions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Elliptical level sets of a\ntwo-dimensional quadratic action with Gaussian widths differing in two\ndirections.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Diagonalizing a quadratic action separates the integral into\nindependently integrable modes.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=(4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle x_1^2\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\operatorname{diag}(4,9)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the formula fail when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a negative eigenvalue?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_1=\\sqrt{\\pi/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The weight grows exponentially in one direction and the real\nintegral diverges.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The formula is a finite Euclidean integral with positive-definite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot a generally defined Minkowski path integral. Functional determinants\nand continuum limits are beyond this lesson.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The Gaussian case clarifies the connection among quadratic actions,\nnormal modes and correlations. Fluctuations can next be modeled as\nstochastic processes evolving in time. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-238-geometry-and-quantum-field-theory-spring-2023/pages/syllabus/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Geometry and Quantum Field Theory, syllabus<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · variational and geometric methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L5 – Gaussian functional integrals and discretized field models · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-513-many-body-theory-for-condensed-matter-systems-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Many-Body Theory for Condensed Matter Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · second quantization and functional integrals; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L5 – Gaussian functional integrals and discretized field models · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-field-theory/\">David Tong – Statistical Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · field theory, correlations and fluctuations; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L5 – Gaussian functional integrals and discretized field models · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M26-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M26-L6 – Langevin dynamics, the Fokker–Planck equation and relaxation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Master: Advanced mathematical methods and model foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · methodological specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: statistical physics; M17:\nnumerical integration; M22: diffusion. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the Fokker–Planck\nequation, stationary variance and relaxation time from a linear\nstochastic model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>Microscopic collisions may make a macroscopic signal irregular.\nInstead of resolving every interaction, a stochastic model separates\nsystematic drift from random fluctuations. A Langevin equation describes\nindividual trajectories; the Fokker–Planck equation describes the\nchanging probability distribution of an ensemble.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process in the Itô sense,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dX_t=-\\gamma X_tdt+\\sqrt{2D}\\,dW_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand Brownian motion\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDrift and diffusion produce the forward equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_tp=\\gamma\\partial_x(xp)+D\\partial_x^2p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSetting the stationary probability current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=-\\gamma xp-D\\partial_xp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto zero yields a normalized Gaussian distribution. Its variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D/\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile the mean displacement relaxes exponentially.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>D<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dX_t=-\\gamma X_t\\,\\mathrm dt+\\sqrt{2D}\\,\\mathrm dW_t,\\quad \\partial_tp=\\gamma\\partial_x(xp)+D\\partial_x^2p,\\quad \\operatorname{Var}_{\\mathrm{stat}}(X)=\\frac D\\gamma.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=2\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.5\\,u^2\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an arbitrary measurement unit, the stationary variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.25\\,u^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the relaxation time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1/\\gamma=0.5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plot shows three independently simulated trajectories and the zero\nline, not experimental measurements.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_142_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three simulated Ornstein–Uhlenbeck time series fluctuating around a zero mean while relaxing.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three simulated Ornstein–Uhlenbeck time\nseries fluctuating around a zero mean while relaxing.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Stochastic paths fluctuate individually; the stationary ensemble\nhas a calculable variance.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate stationary variance for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.8\\,u^2/\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=4/\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dW_t/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot be treated as an ordinary differentiable function?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2\\,u^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The time constant is halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Brownian paths are almost surely nowhere classically differentiable;\nstochastic integration requires its own calculus.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The model assumes additive white noise, linear restoring drift and\ntime-independent coefficients. Discrete simulations approximate the\nstochastic equation; one trajectory does not establish the\ndistribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Langevin and Fokker–Planck descriptions answer different questions\nabout the same process. The final bridge lesson connects temporal\nrelaxation to spectral response. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/kinetic-theory/\">David Tong – Kinetic Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · stochastic processes, Fokker–Planck and kinetic models; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L6 – Langevin dynamics, the Fokker–Planck equation and relaxation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-field-theory/\">David Tong – Statistical Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · field theory, correlations and fluctuations; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L6 – Langevin dynamics, the Fokker–Planck equation and relaxation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M26-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M26-L7 – Methods bridge: eigenmodes, stochastic correlation and model selection<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Master: Advanced mathematical methods and model foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · methodological specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M26-L1 to M26-L6; M13: ensemble\nstatistics; M17: numerical validation. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the time correlation and\npower spectrum of a stationary linear process and articulate the\nassumptions required when transferring mathematics between physical\nmodels.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and observation<\/h2>\n<p>Advanced physics draws on several mathematical tools, but these are\nnot interchangeable labels. Operator theory describes admissible\nspectra, differential geometry organizes local descriptions, variational\nmethods control approximations and stochastic processes model\nfluctuations. Each technique has its own domain of validity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a stationary Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process with drift\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\gamma X_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand diffusion strength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe autocovariance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(\\tau)=(D/\\gamma)e^{-\\gamma|\\tau|}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDefine the two-sided power spectral density as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}C(\\tau)e^{-i\\omega\\tau}d\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegrating the two exponential half-lines yields a Lorentzian. Fourier\nconventions matter: other definitions can move factors of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis correlation spectrum is not automatically the energy spectral\nmeasure of an arbitrary quantum operator.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>D<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mfrac><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(\\tau)=\\frac D\\gamma e^{-\\gamma|\\tau|},\\qquad S(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}C(\\tau)e^{-i\\omega\\tau}\\,\\mathrm d\\tau=\\frac{2D}{\\gamma^2+\\omega^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.25\\,u^2\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none finds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(0)=0.25\\,u^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.092<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(1\\,\\mathrm s)=0.25e^{-1}\\,u^2\\approx0.092\\,u^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe zero-frequency spectral density is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(0)=0.50\\,u^2\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is half this value. The figure compares normalized correlation and\nnormalized power spectrum.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_162_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two plots: normalized autocorrelation decaying with lag and a Lorentzian power spectrum versus frequency.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two plots: normalized autocorrelation\ndecaying with lag and a Lorentzian power spectrum versus\nfrequency.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Time-domain relaxation and frequency-domain width are\nFourier-related descriptions of the same stationary dynamics.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D/\\gamma=0.4\\,u^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the positive half-power frequency of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(\\omega)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor any\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Must an eigenbasis of a self-adjoint quantum operator also\ndiagonalize the Fokker–Planck generator?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4\\,u^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_{1/2}=\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; the operators generally act on different spaces, and the\nFokker–Planck generator is not necessarily self-adjoint in ordinary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The Lorentzian applies to the stationary linear process with the\nspecified two-sided Fourier convention. Nonlinear dynamics, colored\nnoise, nonstationary data and different operator spaces require\nadditional analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The module closes with a verifiable bridge from differential\nequations to fluctuation spectra. Advanced specializations must still\nrecheck domains, symmetries, boundary conditions and empirical\nvalidation. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M27&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/kinetic-theory/\">David Tong – Kinetic Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · stochastic processes, Fokker–Planck and kinetic models; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L7 – Methods bridge: eigenmodes, stochastic correlation and model selection · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-field-theory/\">David Tong – Statistical Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · field theory, correlations and fluctuations; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L7 – Methods bridge: eigenmodes, stochastic correlation and model selection · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-102-introduction-to-functional-analysis-spring-2021/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Introduction to Functional Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Hilbert spaces and spectral theory; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L7 – Methods bridge: eigenmodes, stochastic correlation and model selection · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.davidtong.org/teaching/general-relativity/\">David Tong – General Relativity<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · manifolds, metric and covariant differentiation; Subject relevance to lesson: M26-L7 – Methods bridge: eigenmodes, stochastic correlation and model selection · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
